  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively suggest relevant design principles: arc melting and water quenching can control HEA phase constitution; composition changes can induce Laves or other secondary phases; and magnetic phase separation or antiferromagnetic constituents can reduce saturation magnetization while increasing coercivity. In particular, R17–R19 connect multiphase separation and antiferromagnetic Cr with higher coercivity, while R7, R13, and R16 show composition-driven Laves-phase formation. However, none identifies FeCoNiTi, an fcc+C14 microstructure, Fe/Co/Ni–Ti antiferromagnetic sublattice coupling, the phase-specific transitions, or C14-assisted domain-wall pinning. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but selecting rapid quenching and a C14-dominant Fe27Co27Ni17Ti29 comparator to obtain the reported high-temperature coercivity would require a substantial new idea."
